1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,441
Three weeks into trialling the weekly report agent, Priya was doing exactly what the

2
00:00:05,441 --> 00:00:09,501
C06 checklist asks: reading every output before trusting any of it.

3
00:00:09,501 --> 00:00:14,592
That Monday's report said Brightwell Manufacturing's renewal, eighteen thousand four

4
00:00:14,592 --> 00:00:16,289
hundred dollars, was closed.

5
00:00:16,289 --> 00:00:17,622
Priya knew it was not.

6
00:00:17,622 --> 00:00:21,016
She had spoken to Ravi Chandra herself two days earlier.

7
00:00:21,016 --> 00:00:25,076
Picture the moment properly, because the ordinariness is the point.

8
00:00:25,076 --> 00:00:30,045
Monday, coffee, the drafted report open next to the sheet, her own hand count on a

9
00:00:30,045 --> 00:00:34,651
sticky note, the habit of saying each line out loud before agreeing with it.

10
00:00:34,651 --> 00:00:36,045
Renewals open: matches.

11
00:00:36,045 --> 00:00:37,621
Premium at stake: matches.

12
00:00:37,621 --> 00:00:42,651
Brightwell closed, and the word stopped halfway out of her mouth, because she could

13
00:00:42,651 --> 00:00:44,287
still hear Thursday's call.

14
00:00:44,287 --> 00:00:48,287
Now run the counterfactual that makes this section earn its place.

15
00:00:48,287 --> 00:00:53,317
Without the trial, that report goes to Marcus, Marcus repeats the good news upward,

16
00:00:53,317 --> 00:00:58,226
someone stops watching the one renewal on the book that most needed watching, and

17
00:00:58,226 --> 00:01:02,710
the error surfaces in mid September, at the worst possible moment, wearing

18
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:04,043
everyone's confidence.

19
00:01:04,043 --> 00:01:06,831
The gap between those two Mondays is not luck.

20
00:01:06,831 --> 00:01:07,922
It is a checklist.

21
00:01:08,472 --> 00:01:10,872
The agent did not hallucinate a status.

22
00:01:10,872 --> 00:01:12,473
It did not misread a word.

23
00:01:12,473 --> 00:01:17,581
It read the correct cell, at the wrong position, because a document editing another

24
00:01:17,581 --> 00:01:22,690
client's information shifted every row below it by one, and the agent had no way of

25
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:24,044
knowing that happened.

26
00:01:24,044 --> 00:01:29,091
The deeper distinction is worth one slow beat: position and identity are different

27
00:01:29,091 --> 00:01:32,476
things, and a spreadsheet only really tracks the first.

28
00:01:32,476 --> 00:01:37,461
To a human, "the Brightwell row" means wherever Brightwell's facts happen to live

29
00:01:37,461 --> 00:01:39,861
today, because humans read by identity.

30
00:01:39,861 --> 00:01:44,539
To the sheet, row six is just row six, and an insertion quietly re-addresses

31
00:01:44,539 --> 00:01:45,770
everything below it.

32
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:50,201
The colleague did nothing wrong: inserting a row is what sheets are for.

33
00:01:50,201 --> 00:01:55,064
The agent did nothing wrong: it read what was at the address it was pointed at.

34
00:01:55,064 --> 00:02:00,234
The failure lived entirely in the space between two correct behaviours, which is why

35
00:02:00,234 --> 00:02:03,927
nobody's checklist of mistakes would ever have contained it.

36
00:02:04,477 --> 00:02:09,390
Sit with that distinction, because it is the one most people miss when they think

37
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:10,481
about AI failures.

38
00:02:10,481 --> 00:02:14,909
Every instruction in this course's system prompts was followed correctly.

39
00:02:14,909 --> 00:02:19,034
The failure lived in the data's shape, not in the agent's judgement.

40
00:02:19,034 --> 00:02:21,278
Could a smarter model have caught it?

41
00:02:21,278 --> 00:02:25,160
Ask the question seriously, because the honest no is the lesson.

42
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:30,133
The model can only judge what arrives, never what should have arrived instead, and

43
00:02:30,133 --> 00:02:35,107
what arrived was a perfectly well formed sheet saying a perfectly plausible thing.

44
00:02:35,107 --> 00:02:39,777
It is a family of failures, not a one-off, and it deserves a family portrait.

45
00:02:40,327 --> 00:02:45,221
One signature unites the family, and it is worth memorising: every box green, every

46
00:02:45,221 --> 00:02:48,051
instruction obeyed, and the answer wrong anyway.

47
00:02:48,051 --> 00:02:52,767
No prompt engineering fixes any of these, because nothing in the prompt was ever

48
00:02:52,767 --> 00:02:53,121
wrong.

49
00:02:53,121 --> 00:02:58,014
The defences are structural too, which is the symmetry to notice: cross-checks that

50
00:02:58,014 --> 00:03:02,790
make two fields agree, headers treated as a contract, the unpin ritual, and above

51
00:03:02,790 --> 00:03:07,506
all a human sampling reality against output on a schedule that never quite ends.

52
00:03:08,056 --> 00:03:09,751
Nothing exotic caught this.

53
00:03:09,751 --> 00:03:13,393
No clever monitoring, no second AI checking the first one.

54
00:03:13,393 --> 00:03:18,416
A person, reading every output during trial, who happened to know the truth well

55
00:03:18,416 --> 00:03:21,744
enough to notice a lie that looked exactly like data.

56
00:03:21,744 --> 00:03:26,893
That is what the trust ladder buys you, and it is why Section 7.1 insisted a trial

57
00:03:26,893 --> 00:03:27,835
has to be real.

58
00:03:27,835 --> 00:03:31,979
Count what did not catch it, too, because the list is instructive.

59
00:03:31,979 --> 00:03:37,191
The agent raised nothing: from where it sat, Monday was entirely normal. n8n raised

60
00:03:37,191 --> 00:03:40,142
nothing: every box green, every run successful.

61
00:03:40,142 --> 00:03:45,354
The prompt's own safeguard, never mark closed unless the Status column says so, was

62
00:03:45,354 --> 00:03:46,861
satisfied to the letter.

63
00:03:46,861 --> 00:03:51,947
The only alarm in the entire system was a human who independently knew the truth,

64
00:03:51,947 --> 00:03:57,095
and that is the design lesson hiding in the story: delegation stays safe where you

65
00:03:57,095 --> 00:03:59,356
still hold ground truth of your own.

66
00:03:59,356 --> 00:04:04,128
Priya talks to her clients, so a wrong status has something to collide with.

67
00:04:04,128 --> 00:04:09,151
Hand an agent a domain where you retain no independent knowledge at all, and you

68
00:04:09,151 --> 00:04:10,909
have not delegated the work.

69
00:04:10,909 --> 00:04:12,919
You have delegated the noticing.

70
00:04:13,469 --> 00:04:16,780
Look at how small the fix is, and what kind of fix it is.

71
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:21,660
Not a smarter model, not a longer prompt: one redundancy, two fields that must agree

72
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:23,170
before either is believed.

73
00:04:23,170 --> 00:04:27,876
Cross-checks like that are the cheapest defence in this whole course, and you can

74
00:04:27,876 --> 00:04:30,432
retrofit one onto any agent in an afternoon.

75
00:04:30,432 --> 00:04:32,581
Be honest about what it buys, though.

76
00:04:32,581 --> 00:04:36,880
The name-status check narrows this failure; it does not retire the family.

77
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:41,760
Some future edit will move the sheet in a way no one predicted, which is exactly why

78
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:46,582
the checking never goes to zero, and why the rung this incident happened on, Trial,

79
00:04:46,582 --> 00:04:50,358
is where you want to be standing when your version of it arrives.

80
00:04:50,358 --> 00:04:53,495
That is this section's whole argument in one sentence.

81
00:04:53,495 --> 00:04:58,201
Next lecture turns the discipline that caught this into a rubric you can run even

82
00:04:58,201 --> 00:05:02,964
after an agent has earned enough trust that you are no longer reading every single

83
00:05:02,964 --> 00:05:03,255
line.
